Here is the commit message:
fix(imports): fix a static import bug (#1060)

Here is the root cause:
The scanner determines whether a fully-qualified field/method reference (e.g. `DigestUtil.STREAM_BUFFER_LENGTH`) really needs an import by walking up the reference's qualified-name segments (package/class names) and checking if any of those segments collides with a local field, method, or variable name. The original implementation stored these segments in a `HashSet<String>` (losing ordering) and then iterated over **all** tokens, testing each one against `fieldAndMethodsNames`/`localVariablesOfBlock`. This is semantically wrong because only the **last** token (the actual class/type simple name closest to the field, e.g. "DigestUtil") should be compared against local declarations to decide whether the access is already resolvable without an import. In addition, the package-traversal loop didn't stop before including the top-level (root) package name as an extra token, further polluting the token set. As a result, when a class accessed one of its own private static fields via a fully-qualified name, the algorithm failed to correctly recognize that the field belonged to the enclosing class itself, incorrectly concluding that a static import was required for a field that is already an accessible member of the same class — causing an unnecessary (and technically incorrect, since it's private) static import to be generated.

